# Python gRPC package

Poolster emits these message bindings, typing declarations and client/server
interfaces itself. Generation never invokes protoc, grpcio-tools, Python or a
network service. Serialization uses protobuf 6.33.5; transport uses grpcio 1.78.0.
Install with `python -m pip install .` on Python 3.10 or newer. pyproject.toml pins
runtime dependencies and the setuptools build backend.

`src/<proto-path>_pb2.py` exports native protobuf message classes from retained
wire descriptors. `_pb2.pyi` contains field/constructor typing declarations.
`_pb2_grpc.py` exposes `<Service>Stub`, `<Service>Servicer` and
`add_<Service>Servicer_to_server`; its `.pyi` types synchronous callables and explicit `Async<Service>Stub`,
`Async<Service>Servicer` and async registration APIs.
Import modules using original proto paths, for example
`from store import service_pb2_grpc`. The distribution name does not add an import
namespace: use namespaced proto paths to avoid collisions with installed SDKs.
Runtime descriptor loading uses protobuf's default descriptor pool; incompatible
definitions of the same fully qualified message name cannot coexist.

Implement a generated servicer and register it on `grpc.server(...)`. Construct
stubs with a channel. Unary and all three streaming modes follow grpcio semantics.
Pass `timeout=` and `metadata=` to calls; status errors are `grpc.RpcError`.
Cancellation and transport credentials use the runtime APIs. No credentials,
automatic retry policy, server implementation or connection is generated.
Use the separate Async bindings with `grpc.aio` channels and servers.
`client_<distribution>.py` exposes raw service_method functions, FlatClient native
callable attributes, and GroupedClient service groups. Client selects the configured
flat or idiomatic facade; raw style leaves that alias absent. All facades preserve
native call options and streaming call cancellation.

Independent generation options control typing declarations, client/server modules,
bounded binary helpers, ProtoJSON helpers and reflection helpers. Native Message
methods remain available through the protobuf runtime. Extra helpers enforce byte
and depth bounds; maxDepth above the native decoder ceiling of 100 is rejected.
Unknown fields are preserved by default and may be discarded in bounded decoders.
Native channel injection supports TLS, credentials and grpcio interceptors.

Presence, maps, oneofs, defaults, required proto2 fields, extensions and wire
numbers stay in native protobuf descriptors. Runtime sources, messages, stubs and
typing declarations are separate files. Sources and FileDescriptorSet bytes
(encoded as a JSON integer array) remain under `proto/`. Reconstruct a binary
FileDescriptorSet with `bytes(json.load(open("proto/descriptor-set.json")))`.
Editions, Python keyword public names, unsafe/case-colliding module paths, custom
files in runtime-owned `google/protobuf/`, and conflicting exports are rejected.
Regeneration uses normal Poolster ownership/customization rules.

With `removeEnumPrefix`, enum constants such as `COLOR_RED` gain a `RED`
alias at their native module or containing message scope. The original constants,
descriptor names and wire numbers remain canonical; aliases that collide with
existing exports, fields or native message methods are rejected before emission.

With `immutableViews`, `views_<package>.freeze(message)` returns a deep snapshot
with a schema-specific `Frozen_<qualified_message_name>` type. Its scalar fields
are read-only properties, repeated fields are tuples, maps are read-only mappings,
and nested messages have the same immutable field annotations. `copy_native()`
returns an independent mutable native message.

To emit a declaration-only package, enable `emitTypes` and disable `emitBinary`,
`emitJson`, `emitClients`, `emitServers` and `emitReflection`. This produces `.pyi`
bindings without executable message modules; use compatible separately supplied
native bindings when executing code described by those declarations.
